WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
We'll walk through validation gates, how to structure retry loops that actually improve

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.239
results, and when retrying is the wrong answer. We'll also cover retry budgets and backoff

3
00:00:10.239 --> 00:00:14.560
strategies. A lot of pipeline reliability lives here, so let's get into it.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.040
Let's anchor the lesson with three terms. A validation gate is a deterministic check that

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.000
sits between the model's output and your application. It decides whether to accept the

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.560
output, retry, or escalate to a human or error handler. A retry loop is the mechanism that

7
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.759
re-prompts the model after a failed gate check. Critically, it includes the failure reason,

8
00:00:37.759 --> 00:00:43.520
so the model has something concrete to act on. And a retry budget is the hard cap on how many

9
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.639
attempts the system allows before giving up. These three concepts work together. The gate

10
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:53.599
defines pass and fail, the loop handles the retry, and the budget keeps the whole thing

11
00:00:53.599 --> 00:00:59.840
from running forever. Keep these definitions in mind as we go deeper. There are three distinct

12
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.440
layers of validation, and you need all of them. Schema validation is the first layer. It checks

13
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.800
that the output has the right fields, the right types, and the right structure. This is fast,

14
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.959
cheap, and fully deterministic. Format validation goes one level deeper. It checks that field values

15
00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:22.160
meet specific constraints. A date field should hold a date. A score field should be a number

16
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.000
within a defined range. Semantic validation is the hardest layer. It checks that the content

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.519
actually means what it should. Does the summary actually summarize the right thing? Does the

18
00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:39.440
recommendation align with the input constraints? Each layer catches a different class of failure.

19
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.160
Running only one of them leaves you exposed to the failures the other two are designed to catch.

20
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.519
I want to make the distinction between schema and semantic validation concrete,

21
00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:54.800
because teams almost always implement schema checks first and stop there. Schema validation

22
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.919
is structural. You can check it with a JSON schema or a Pydantic model in milliseconds

23
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:05.360
without calling the model again. It will catch missing fields and wrong types. It won't catch

24
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.479
a summary that summarizes the wrong thing or a recommendation that completely contradicts

25
00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:16.639
the user's constraints. That's where semantic validation comes in. Semantic validation is slower

26
00:02:16.639 --> 00:02:22.160
and often requires a second model call or a handwritten rule set, but it's the layer that

27
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.440
catches the failures your users actually notice. Think of schema validation as the floor and

28
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.399
semantic validation as the ceiling. You need both to build a system users can trust.

29
00:02:33.679 --> 00:02:38.960
One of the most important details in retry loop design is how you frame the retry prompt.

30
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.520
A generic try again prompt almost always reproduces the original failure.

31
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.360
The model doesn't know what it got wrong, so it makes the same choices again.

32
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.039
Instead, include the failure reason explicitly. Something like, your previous response was

33
00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:58.080
missing the required summary field. Here is the output you returned. Please revise it to include

34
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.160
a one sentence summary. That kind of specificity gives the model something to act on. Paste the

35
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.279
original output alongside the failure message so the model can compare what it produced with what

36
00:03:09.279 --> 00:03:15.279
you needed. Concrete feedback drives concrete improvement. Vague feedback drives vague revisions

37
00:03:15.279 --> 00:03:21.279
that look different on the surface but fail the same validation check again. Retry budgets aren't

38
00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:27.039
optional. Without a hard cap, a validation failure can turn into an infinite loop that drains cost

39
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:32.880
and blocks the pipeline. So let's talk about how to design a budget well. Set a small cap.

40
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.160
Two to three attempts covers the vast majority of recoverable failures. If a model can't succeed in

41
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.919
three tries, the problem is almost certainly a prompt design issue, not a one-off hiccup. Log

42
00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:49.919
every attempt. Logging what failed and why on each retry is how you identify systemic problems in your

43
00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:56.240
prompts over time. And escalate explicitly when the budget runs out. Don't silently return an

44
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.000
empty result. Route to a human review queue or return a structured error that downstream systems

45
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.720
can handle. Silence is the most dangerous failure mode of all. Sometimes a model gets stuck in a

46
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.000
pattern. It produces output that fails the same validation check on every attempt with only

47
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.000
superficial differences. Identical retries don't help here. What you need is variance. One technique

48
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.160
is a small temperature bump on each retry. Start the first retry at the model's default temperature.

49
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.480
Raise it slightly on the second attempt to push the model toward different phrasing and structure.

50
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.640
Another technique is prompt variation. Reframe the instruction slightly on each retry.

51
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.440
Not enough to change what you're asking for, but enough to break the model out of the same failure

52
00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.359
path. Exponential backoff is also important when you're hitting rate limits under failure conditions.

53
00:04:47.359 --> 00:04:52.559
Don't hammer the API with rapid retries. Space them out so you don't add a rate limit failure

54
00:04:52.559 --> 00:04:59.279
on top of a validation failure. This slide introduces additional vocabulary worth knowing.

55
00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:03.760
The reviewer pattern is a second model call that critiques the first call's output

56
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.239
before your application ever sees it. We'll cover this in depth in the next lesson,

57
00:05:08.239 --> 00:05:12.160
but it's worth naming here because it's the next level up from a simple retry loop.

58
00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:18.880
The escalation path is the defined action you take when retries run out. This should be explicit

59
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.880
in your architecture. Either return a structured error, flag the item for human review, or fall

60
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.239
back to a known safe default. Don't leave it undefined. And semantic drift is something to

61
00:05:30.239 --> 00:05:35.839
watch for in longer retry chains. If you run too many retries without a strong anchor, the output

62
00:05:35.839 --> 00:05:41.279
can satisfy your format checks, but drift further from the intended meaning with each attempt.

63
00:05:41.279 --> 00:05:47.839
More retries isn't always better. Knowing when to escalate instead of retrying is a judgment call,

64
00:05:47.839 --> 00:05:53.519
but there are clear signals. The most reliable signal is a semantic failure that repeats across

65
00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:58.320
multiple attempts. If the model produces structurally valid output that consistently

66
00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.839
misses the intended meaning, that's not a random failure. That's a signal your prompt isn't giving

67
00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:09.920
the model what it needs to succeed. Escalate when that pattern appears. Another signal is when the

68
00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.880
format passes, but the content meaning is consistently wrong. Schema checks give you a

69
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.799
green light, but the actual answer is off. That's a semantic validation failure, and no number of

70
00:06:20.799 --> 00:06:26.480
retries is likely to fix it without changing the prompt. Treat repeated semantic failures as design

71
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.959
feedback, not bad luck. They're telling you something important about your instruction design.

72
00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:36.880
Let me leave you with the single most common validation mistake I see in production systems.

73
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.359
Teams implement schema validation. They confirm the output is valid JSON with the right fields,

74
00:06:43.359 --> 00:06:49.760
and they ship. But schema-only validation gives false confidence. A JSON object can be perfectly

75
00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.440
structured and completely wrong. A required field can contain a string that technically satisfies

76
00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.880
the type check but says the opposite of what was intended. You need at least one semantic check

77
00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.320
on top of your schema checks. It doesn't have to be a full second model call. Sometimes a simple

78
00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.760
rule works. Does the output mention at least one of the required topics? Is the score within a

79
00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:17.839
plausible range? Passing schema validation is the floor. Build checks that actually catch the

80
00:07:17.839 --> 00:07:23.600
failures your users will notice. Reliable pipelines need all three validation layers,

81
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:29.679
not just schema checks. Cap your retries, include the failure reason in every retry prompt,

82
00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:35.359
and escalate when the same semantic failure repeats across attempts. Build your budgets

83
00:07:35.359 --> 00:07:40.959
before you deploy, not after the first runaway loop. And that covers validation gates. In the

84
00:07:40.959 --> 00:07:46.239
next lesson, we'll look at multi-pass review, where a second model call critiques the first

85
00:07:46.239 --> 00:07:49.359
before the user ever sees it. I'll see you in that lesson.

